WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:02.202
Start in the branch list on the left.

00:02.202 --> 00:04.625
I'll click Find a branch and type observation.

00:04.625 --> 00:07.413
That narrows the list to the notebook change.

00:07.413 --> 00:10.139
Click Add a notebook for field observations.

00:10.139 --> 00:12.119
That click opens the review.

00:12.119 --> 00:15.149
Branch Review prepares the original and changed previews,

00:15.149 --> 00:19.000
and the list on the left fills with the checks for this commit.

00:19.000 --> 00:21.172
The application opens in the middle.

00:21.172 --> 00:22.998
I'll move up to Original and click it.

00:22.998 --> 00:26.361
Here's the starting point, a page with one Add a note button.

00:26.361 --> 00:29.110
Now follow the pointer across to Changed.

00:29.110 --> 00:31.782
The proposed version has an observation field,

00:31.782 --> 00:33.550
a button to record an entry,

00:33.550 --> 00:37.740
and a list underneath. I can switch back and forth without touching

00:37.740 --> 00:40.000
the branch in my own working directory.

00:40.000 --> 00:42.237
Where did the checks on the left come from?

00:42.237 --> 00:44.245
Open Settings in the top right.

00:44.245 --> 00:46.869
Under Coding agent, this project uses Codex CLI.

00:46.869 --> 00:48.331
When I opened the branch,

00:48.331 --> 00:50.444
Branch Review collected the changed files,

00:50.444 --> 00:52.928
the Git diff, and the project scenario.

00:52.928 --> 00:56.503
It started the installed Codex command line program in a separate

00:56.503 --> 00:59.832
checkout with read-only access. Codex uses that material to identify

00:59.832 --> 01:02.000
things a reviewer can actually try.

01:02.000 --> 01:04.408
Close Settings to get back to the review.

01:04.408 --> 01:06.050
Click a title in the left column.

01:06.050 --> 01:08.645
Watch the panel on the right change with it.

01:08.645 --> 01:11.557
Each item tells you what to do in the running application and what

01:11.557 --> 01:13.255
you should expect to see.

01:13.255 --> 01:15.756
Codex returned these items in a structured plan,

01:15.756 --> 01:18.369
and Branch Review saved that plan for this revision.

01:18.369 --> 01:22.000
You can move between checks in any order by clicking their titles.

01:22.000 --> 01:25.331
Let's try the journal. I'll click the observation field,

01:25.331 --> 01:26.570
type a note about the ridge,

01:26.570 --> 01:30.559
and click Record entry. The new note appears above the sample note,

01:30.559 --> 01:33.716
the field clears, and the app confirms the save.

01:33.716 --> 01:36.950
Now I'll click Record entry again with the field empty.

01:36.950 --> 01:39.642
The message changes to Write an observation first.

01:39.642 --> 01:42.000
The list still has the same two entries.

01:42.000 --> 01:44.762
There's one thing I'd change before approving this.

01:44.762 --> 01:46.210
The field says New observation,

01:46.210 --> 01:47.791
but the button says Record entry.

01:47.791 --> 01:50.324
Watch the pointer go back to that button.

01:50.324 --> 01:53.162
I right-click it directly inside the preview.

01:53.162 --> 01:55.124
That opens the change request.

01:55.124 --> 01:58.381
The attached screenshot shows the screen I was looking at,

01:58.381 --> 02:02.000
and the selected element tells the agent which button I meant.

02:02.000 --> 02:04.678
Click in the request field and describe the edit.

02:04.678 --> 02:06.503
Rename Record entry to Save observation,

02:06.503 --> 02:08.328
and keep the save behavior unchanged.

02:08.328 --> 02:10.290
Now scroll down inside this dialog.

02:10.290 --> 02:13.104
Click Add to queue. That saves the instruction,

02:13.104 --> 02:15.816
the screenshot, and the selected button together.

02:15.816 --> 02:19.159
I can finish reviewing the other items before handing the request

02:19.159 --> 02:20.000
to Codex.

02:20.000 --> 02:23.330
I use the list on the left to pick up each remaining check.

02:23.330 --> 02:25.123
I'll save a second entry with Enter,

02:25.123 --> 02:28.252
try the blank field, and remove the saved entries.

02:28.252 --> 02:31.747
After each test I move to the right-hand panel and click Looks good

02:31.747 --> 02:35.132
and Next. Watch the completed count on the left.

02:35.132 --> 02:38.882
The check I passed changes status while the item with my fix request

02:38.882 --> 02:41.572
stays flagged. Once every item has an outcome,

02:41.572 --> 02:44.115
Review results shows my queued request.

02:44.115 --> 02:47.000
Click Apply one queued change to send it to Codex.

02:47.000 --> 02:49.776
The planning job could only read the code.

02:49.776 --> 02:53.095
The fix job runs in an isolated checkout where Codex can edit files.

02:53.095 --> 02:55.549
Branch Review commits the change.

02:55.549 --> 02:59.773
When it's ready, click through to review the update.

02:59.773 --> 03:03.113
I'll go back to the button and try saving another observation.

03:03.113 --> 03:04.883
The label now reads Save observation,

03:04.883 --> 03:07.055
and the new note appears in the list.

03:07.055 --> 03:10.000
That checks both halves of the request.

03:10.000 --> 03:12.236
Field Notes keeps entries in browser storage.

03:12.236 --> 03:14.894
What if the application writes to a real database?

03:14.894 --> 03:17.500
Here's a recorded review of a Meta Clinic intake form.

03:17.500 --> 03:20.862
Both sides start from a known scenario with fictional records.

03:20.862 --> 03:24.383
We want to answer two questions: can we save an answer in the changed

03:24.383 --> 03:27.728
version without affecting Original, and will that answer still be

03:27.728 --> 03:30.000
there after the desktop app restarts?

03:30.000 --> 03:33.429
For this Rails project, the preview adapter assigns the two checkouts

03:33.429 --> 03:37.042
separate runtime slots. Each slot has its own PostgreSQL container

03:37.042 --> 03:40.619
and port. The database name includes the side of the comparison and

03:40.619 --> 03:42.905
the first twelve characters of the Git commit.

03:42.905 --> 03:46.315
When Rails starts, the adapter points it at that database.

03:46.315 --> 03:50.426
The two screens look alike because they started with comparable records,

03:50.426 --> 03:53.000
not because they share a live patient record.

03:53.000 --> 03:54.705
Watch the saved intake on Changed.

03:54.705 --> 03:57.281
The reviewer edited the reason for the visit,

03:57.281 --> 03:59.345
and the form advanced to revision eight.

03:59.345 --> 04:01.864
That's a revision of the intake record,

04:01.864 --> 04:04.364
distinct from the Git commit above it.

04:04.364 --> 04:06.448
Original retained its seeded answer.

04:06.448 --> 04:10.407
And here is Changed reopened after a desktop restart: the edited answer

04:10.407 --> 04:14.517
is still present. That verifies both separation and persistence for

04:14.517 --> 04:16.000
this project's adapter.

04:16.000 --> 04:19.810
Back in Field Notes, I'll finish the checks for the updated version

04:19.810 --> 04:23.560
and open Review results. The review lists what passed.

04:23.560 --> 04:27.230
Approval is a separate action tied to the commit and scenario we just

04:27.230 --> 04:31.678
checked. Branch Review checks the remote again before delivery.

04:31.678 --> 04:34.072
If main has moved and the combined code changes,

04:34.072 --> 04:38.000
the reviewer has to check that new version before it can ship.

04:38.000 --> 04:41.689
With this version approved, click Merge and push to main.

04:41.689 --> 04:45.437
The confirmation identifies the commit and the remote branch.

04:45.437 --> 04:48.478
This example uses a local Git remote created for the demo.

04:48.478 --> 04:53.030
Branch Review pushes the commit and checks the remote's copy of main.

04:53.030 --> 04:55.876
The Merged and pushed message is the receipt.

04:55.876 --> 05:00.000
The commit beside Changed is the same one that reached the remote.
